==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|tanþs|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|h₃dónts||tooth|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognates with Old Frisian {{term|toth|tōth|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|tand|lang=osx}}, Dutch {{term|tand|lang=nl}}, Old High German {{term|zan|lang=goh}}, {{term|zand|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Zahn|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|tǫnn|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|tand|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐍄𐌿𐌽𐌸𐌿𐍃|lang=got}}; and with Sanskrit {{term|दन्त|lang=sa}} (Hindi {{term|दांत़|lang=hi}}), Ancient Greek {{term|ὀδών|lang=grc}}, Latin {{term|dens|lang=la}} (French {{term|dent|lang=fr}}), Old Irish {{term|dét|lang=sga}}, Lithuanian {{term|dantis|dantìs|lang=lt}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/toːθ/|lang=ang}}

